# Phrase substitutions applied to the carved tree, after file selection and before
# the patches.
#
# WHY A TABLE AND NOT PATCHES. These are one-phrase edits scattered across twenty
# comments. As context diffs they would break every time someone edits a neighbouring
# line in the development repository -- a mechanism that fails whenever a colleague
# writes a good comment is the wrong mechanism. A phrase substitution survives its
# surroundings changing.
#
# WHY THEY EXIST AT ALL, and this is the part worth getting right: `ConMin` in this
# repository is not a rival paper's coined term, it is the name of a package that sits
# next to ConGen. A comment pointing at a sibling module is correct documentation here.
# It becomes wrong only in the artifact, which does not ship that module -- there the
# name is a pointer to something absent, exactly like a stale file path. So the fix
# belongs on the artifact side, and the upstream comments stay accurate.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Rules apply in file order, so a longer phrase must precede any
# shorter phrase contained in it -- otherwise the short rule consumes the text and the
# long one silently never fires. The match-at-least-once assertion is what surfaces
# that: two shadowed rules were removed the first time it ran.
#
# FORMAT: one `find<TAB>replace` per line. Blank lines and # comments ignored.
# Every entry must match at least once, and no `find` may survive: carve.sh asserts
# both. An entry that matches nothing means the upstream wording moved, and the table
# needs updating rather than quietly doing nothing.

